## Description

Demonstrates `\method`, `\S3method`, and `\S4method` usage entries, including operator generics reformatted as infix expressions.

## Usage

```r
# S3 method for class 'data.frame'
print(x, ...)

# Default S3 method
print(x, ...)

# S4 method for signature 'MyClass'
show(object)

# S4 method for signature 'OldClass,NewClass'
coerce(from, to)

# S3 method for class 'myclass'
print(x, ...)

# S3 method for class 'myclass'
e1 + e2

# S3 method for class 'myclass'
x[i, j, ..., drop = TRUE]

# S3 method for class 'myclass'
x[[i, ...]]

# S3 method for class 'myclass'
x$name

# S3 method for class 'myclass'
e1 == e2

# S3 method for class 'myclass'
lhs %>% rhs

arrange(.data, ...)

# S3 method for class 'data.frame'
arrange(.data, ..., .by_group = FALSE)

# Default S3 method
arrange(.data, ...)

# S3 method for class 'tbl_df'
print(x, ..., n = NULL, width = NULL)

# S3 method for class 'myclass'
summary(object, ...)
```

## Arguments

::: {.list-table header-rows=1}

- - Argument
  - Description

- - `x`
  - An object to print.

- - `...`
  - Additional arguments passed to methods.

- - `object`
  - An object to show or summarize.

- - `from, to`
  - Objects to coerce between classes.

- - `e1, e2`
  - Objects for the operator method.

- - `i, j`
  - Indices for subsetting.

- - `drop`
  - Whether to simplify the result.

- - `name`
  - The name of the element to extract.

- - `lhs, rhs`
  - Left- and right-hand side operands.

- - `.data`
  - A data frame to arrange.

- - `.by_group`
  - Whether to sort by grouping columns first.

- - `n, width`
  - Number of rows and display width.

:::

## Value

Nothing of interest; this fixture only exercises `\usage` rendering.

## Examples

```{r}
methods(class = "myclass")
```
